From eeebe56239e0c72ee8bc1f8cf6d388143ce99e4c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 23:54:26 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 192/209] feat(area): spline shape component (legacy Bezier
 rule, closed flag)

The fourth shape kind, and the first that is a path rather than a volume.
Decision 04: point model A1, interpolation I1, closed flag C2, local space.

SplineMath.h ports CSplineObject's maths as free functions over caller-owned
arrays: GetBezierPos (SplineObject.cpp:750-757), GetBezierTangent (:759-771),
GetBezierSegmentLength (:773-787, the same 32 chords), BezierAnglesCorrection
(:896-958), GetLocalBezierNormal's roll blend (:841-885) and
CRoadObject::GetLocalWidth's width blend (RoadObject.cpp:125-151, which is a
plain lerp - legacy computes `ed` there and then throws it away).

The handles are derived, never stored, so the point list is the whole file
format - which CGravityVolumeObject already proves lossless by writing its
points with Back/Forw commented out (GravityVolumeObject.cpp:810-811).

One deliberate difference in the derivation ORDER. Legacy's rule is not a pure
function of the positions: the two end points read the neighbour's
already-computed handle while interior points depend on positions only, and
legacy walks the list in index order, so index 0 reads what point 1 held from
the previous pass. Because the handles are serialized and re-corrected on every
load (:1219-1220), what that converges on is the fixed point. DeriveHandles()
computes all interior points first and the ends afterwards, reaching the same
fixed point in one pass, independent of edit history. A closed spline has no end
points at all: every point uses the interior rule with wrapped neighbours, so
the closing segment joins the first one smoothly.

CSplineShapeComponent stores {pos, width, angle, defaultWidth, obstructSound}
per point plus Closed and Offset, and implements the whole contract: no interior
unless closed (a path has none), DistanceToHull as the distance to the sampled
curve (negative inside a closed one), IntersectRay as a closest-approach test
with a shape-declared tolerance, GetContour as the sampled curve at legacy's 8
samples per segment (:1266), and EditorHitTest as CSplineObject::HitTest
(:1376-1428) - the curve, chord by chord, never a volume.

A lazily rebuilt cache holds the handles, the same data in world space and a
per-segment arc-length table. The world copy is the local one put THROUGH the
matrix rather than re-derived: a Bezier is affine invariant, whereas the handle
rule uses chord lengths and would bend differently under a non-uniform scale.

No CalcBBox origin re-basing (SplineObject.cpp:1208-1217): the entity transform
owns the placement, so no point edit moves the entity. The inspector action is
"Recenter Points" and not "Recenter Pivot" on purpose - EditorQt's CEntityObject
owns the transform and never reads it back off the entity, so a component
calling SetPos would be overwritten by the object's next push. It rebases the
points on their centroid and compensates with Offset instead, leaving the curve
exactly where it is; moving the pivot belongs to an editor-side action. Its
callback captures an EntityId and the component GUID, never `this`.

IShapeComponent.h gains, append-only: ISplineShape::GetLocalWidth /
GetLocalBezierNormal / ParamToSegment for the consumers, and
IShapeComponentEdit::GetEdgePoints with a default of 0 meaning "this edge is the
plain chord", which every other kind inherits unchanged.

The Spline slot in ShapeKinds.h was already declared in stage 1c, so the
pairwise incompatibility with Box, Sphere and Polygon came for free.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
